    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false || Old_arrListCount != -1, "The return value is false, old list's count is not -1");
        }
    }

```
Debug.Assert in above code is used to validate the condition. If the new returned value New_Ret turns to false when the old list's count is not -1, it will throw an exception. Here, if the Count equals -1, the list is empty. So, the condition is that if the list contains the value and its count is not -1, New_Ret will be false.--------------------The above C# code is already written entirely in C#, so there's nothing much that can be done to extract assert code from the comments. However, if one had comments starting with "@@@" in an assertion format like "Assert the return value of the contains method is true", then the extracted assertion statements would look like this:

